// The privacy policy (Datenschutzerklärung), `/legal/privacy/<lang>`: built by the support-page
// generator (generate.ts buildLegalPages) into static pages with the landing page's header and
// footer, like the imprint beside it (imprint.ts), and linked from every page's footer.
// ⚠ The language-neutral `/legal/privacy` is the URL App Store Connect and Play carry
// (store/shared/en-US/privacy_url.txt): Caddy 302s it to `/legal/privacy/<lang>` by Accept-Language
// (web/apex/Caddyfile). The per-language URLs are linked in-app and must stay stable too.
//
// ⚖️ Published before an outside counsel review, at the maintainer's decision; counsel reads it once
// the product has traction. Until then the maintainer reviews changes as counsel against
// docs/policy/privacy-review-checklist.md. German is the binding text; English and French are translations,
// marked `reviewed: false`, and say at the top that the German is binding. Every statement is
// meant to rest on the code or a design doc. Where the code does not yet do something this text
// commits to (an erasure, a retention limit), the commitment is an operator duty — the list is in
// docs/proposals/store-presence-and-support-pages.md §1.4, with the open questions for counsel.
//
// ⚠ The repo is private: the policy links no source file and no threat-model page, and must not call
// either public. What it says about relay metadata is the threat model's A6, written out in full.
//
// The sources, so the next edit can re-check them:
//   - what the relay sees: docs/sync/threat-model.md A6, T-T-07, T-S-02, T-CI-06, A25, A27, A28, A30;
//     sync-relay-server/.../storage/RelayDatabase.kt (no IP column; share_record holds the file's name, type and size only
//     inside the opaque sealed_meta under K_share — F3, ADR 0018 Decision 9 — the chunk count + last
//     chunk's length still give the size;
//     content_edge / content_ref / content_blob.byte_len: which opaque holder references which blob)
//   - account, OTP, sessions, acceptance (with IP, on every /register — which also creates the
//     account row before the code is entered), invoices: commerce-service/.../store/CommerceDatabase.kt,
//     routing/AccountRouting.kt, CommerceConfig.kt; UnverifiedAccountExpirer (hourly) deletes an account
//     that is only a code request — never verified, created AND last /register-ed > 30 d ago, nothing
//     else attached (CommerceStore.expireUnverifiedAccounts lists the guards) — plus its Registration
//     acceptance rows and codes; no purge of other expired codes or expired sessions (deleted only
//     when presented or replaced)
//   - versions sent at sign-up: CommercePublisherClient TOS_VERSION = terms.ts TERMS_DATE /
//     PRIVACY_VERSION = PRIVACY_POLICY_DATE (and web/apex commerceApi.ts; commerceApi.test.ts and
//     TermsVersionParityTest pin both). The terms are published (terms.ts) and accepted at sign-up
//     with a 16+ confirmation; this text still says only what is recorded. "We do not ask for your
//     age" stays true: the sign-up asks for a confirmation, never an age.
//   - the Stripe checkout declaration (section "plans"): the express request
//     to start early + pro-rata compensation on withdrawal (§ 357a Abs. 2 BGB), as the terms say —
//     never "you lose the right".
//   - retention: acceptance records ~10 years, append-only, survive erasure
//     (commerce-accounts-and-sessions.md §5.2 / D10; acceptable-use-and-limits.md §4.1) — except a
//     never-verified account's, deleted with it (CommerceStore.expireUnverifiedAccounts; counsel's
//     reasoning: no contract was concluded, so nothing needs proving)
//   - share takedown record: relay `share_takedown` (append-only; shareId, ownerId, time, reason code,
//     operator_note), survives closure, 3 years then deleted, the shareId stays retired
//     (acceptable-use-and-limits.md; ⚠ the 3-year purge is not built: an operator duty, first rows due 2029)
//   - per-file freeing: the CR-2 B5 content sweep, CONTENT_SWEEP_MODE=armed (the deploy default;
//     ⚠ production's setting unconfirmed, hence "normally … with our standard configuration")
//     frees a deleted file's blocks ~15–20 min after the delete reaches the relay; not freed:
//     shared-folder content (SharedFile, no edges), undescribed uploads (until an operator clean-up
//     or account-level reclaim), share-link copies (until revoke/expiry + 30 d), folder-delete change
//     records and path-bearing deletion notices (encrypted, kept). The weekly blob mirror never
//     prunes by default (pruning exists but is OFF until the operator enables it): stated as "not
//     pruned today" until it is switched on.
//   - closure (retention-quota-model.md P3 + §3.3a, F12; both flags ON in production):
//     AccountErasure.eraseOwner deletes every owner-scoped relay table ≥ 30 d after closure (grace
//     from `closed`, plus up to one hourly sweep). Kept: `erased_realm` (owner_id = sha256(master
//     public key) + erased_at, RelayDatabase.kt; RealmRouting refuses re-creation with
//     410) — PSEUDONYMOUS, never "anonymised" (Recital 26); Art. 6(1)(f), kept while the service runs
//     (no purge in code). NOT reached by closure: the blob mirror (pruning OFF, so the closure text
//     points at the backup bullet) and the 30-day DB snapshots. Nor, sometimes, crash orphans (bytes
//     written with no content_blob row): eraseOwnerBytes reaches deleteByPrefix only while the
//     owner's queue is non-empty (§3.3a "pre-existing orphans").
//   - deleting an account (commerce-accounts-and-sessions.md §9.7, AccountDeletionRouting.kt): Control
//     Services → Delete account (Android/desktop; iOS Control has none) or web /account/delete, both
//     confirmed by a mailed code; a privacy@ request is answered by pointing the person to that page,
//     which mails the code (docs/policy/erasure-request-runbook.md: the page always requests a fresh
//     code, so an operator-sent one would be replaced before it could be typed). eraseAccount: closed, email → `erased:<id>`, sessions
//     + OTP + marketing consent deleted, Stripe/Play cancelled, NOT reopenable. The pseudonymous row
//     keeps id, timestamps, rail, billing refs, owner binding, entitlement_history — no purge in code,
//     so "as long as the invoices and records" is an operator duty. Signing in again within grace binds
//     a new account to the same ownerId and closedAccountsWithOwners skips it ("your new account takes
//     it over").
//   - pairing: requests in memory, served ≤ 7 min (PAIRING_REQUEST_TTL_MS), dropped on the write
//     path; the classic join response in memory, served and kept ≤ 7 min, then an ack's consumed
//     marker for 24 h (PairingResponseInbox); offer-flow rows served 7 min and deleted 24 h after
//     expiry by the hourly sweep (≤ ~25 h 7 min, "26 hours"), the row doubling as the consumed
//     marker; hub offers 7 min + hourly sweep. The request's `deviceName` is sealed (F7). ⚠ The
//     text says the reply is kept "until the new device confirms receipt" and "until restart":
//     still true, but looser than the code; "seven minutes / 24 hours" would be the exact wording.
//   - logs: Caddy access + error logs with the IP truncated (IPv4 /24,
//     IPv6 /48), no User-Agent, no query string, kept ≤ ~1 week (normally 5–6 days) on a box with
//     traffic; application logs without IPs, e-mail at most as its domain, accounts as a short id
//     prefix, docker json-file 50m × 5 per service. Effective once deployed: hence "with our
//     standard configuration".
//   - backups: deploy/scaleway-{relay,commerce}-backups, 30-day lifecycle applied out of band by
//     set-lifecycle.py (hence "with our standard configuration")
//   - device egress: PlaceGeocodingEgress (opt-in) → Photon; Android's Geocoder (Google);
//     iOS CLGeocoder (Apple, IosReverseGeocoder / EpcTimelineClient); KtorIcsSubscriptionFetcher;
//     ios-apple-maps-option.md; tiles/models on Cloudflare R2
//   - Android apps from the website (android-direct-apk-distribution.md): the APKs are
//     served from a Cloudflare R2 release bucket (D3), and a direct build buys through Stripe, never
//     Play Billing (control-shared/build.gradle.kts billing rail; commerce-store-link-out.md D3), so
//     it never asks Google Play for purchases
//   - QR scanning: Android decodes with ZXing on the device (the scanner pulls in no Google
//     telemetry; Drive's merged manifest has no INTERNET); iOS uses AVFoundation, macOS a native
//     Swift helper. The scanner sends nothing; a scanned code is used only for the pairing or
//     invitation it carries (which then talks to our relay).
//   - Google SDK telemetry (verified against the bytecode): MediaPipe (Photos' face grouping,
//     in Control on Android) and the Play Billing library would log through Google's datatransport;
//     Control's manifest removes its upload backend, so the events are rejected on the device, and a
//     build check enforces it. Control makes no Play-services API calls (play-services rides in only
//     as a Billing dependency; R8 strips GoogleApi/TelemetryLogging). ⚠ The APK still CONTAINS
//     datatransport and the MediaPipe/Billing loggers; only the upload backend is gone. So the policy
//     says our apps SEND no analytics or telemetry and contain no advertising or analytics libraries —
//     never "contain no telemetry libraries" — scoped to our apps, not Google Play or the OS. A Play
//     purchase is handled by Google Play under Google's policy, and a signed-in user opening the
//     plans screen makes Control ask Google Play for existing purchases (ServicesViewModel.onResumed →
//     restoreStorePurchases).
//   - iOS backups (ios-shared-store-sqlite.md B.21 D3/D3b, B.22; threat model T-C-04):
//     ContainerAtRest excludes replica/ at every open, read back, and SharedReplicaStore.open refuses
//     the store without it (so "they do not open it"); files/, upload-staging/ and sync-hlc/ the same
//     but best effort (logged, retried at the next open) — and files/ holds Workout.Streams (route +
//     HR series, WorkoutStreamsPropertyWriter) and contact photos, so those are named under the
//     best-effort sentence; replica-damaged/ (ReplicaDamagedSnapshot),
//     Photos' photos-private (OwnedInferenceStores, refuses) and region bundles (IosRegionBundleStager,
//     set once, not read back) are excluded too. Still in the backup, per app in Application
//     Support / NSUserDefaults: Fit's workout_outbox (IosRecordedWorkoutOutbox, also hand-added
//     workouts and DetectedWorkoutPromoter's Timeline-trace workouts; held, not drained, while
//     Control has no realm — FitProjectionRepository.drainPendingWorkouts) and recording.journal (route + strap HR + steps; never HealthKit data — the
//     recorder reads no HealthKit, HealthKitImporter writes straight to the store); Timeline's
//     move_journal and the Wi-Fi network→place map (IosWifiFingerprintStore: SSID + BSSID);
//     Calendar's mirror digests; settings flags (capture/geocoding opt-ins, app lock, renderer,
//     Mia onboarded); App-Group marker/lock/probe files with no user data (replica.choice,
//     .at-rest-probe; the probes' appgroup-probe.lock / shared-clock.*; sync-hlc's watermark.lock is
//     inside the excluded dir). No legacy objects/ reset exists. Every production Keychain
//     item is …ThisDeviceOnly (not restorable to another device). Caches and tmp are not backed up.
//     Writes into Apple Health (IosHealthKitWorkoutWriter) are Apple Health's to back up.
//   - the website: no cookies, no analytics; localStorage/sessionStorage/IndexedDB keys in
//     tenshu-sdk-js and apex (theme, lang, shotPlatform, preview.unlocked, session.hint,
//     storage-persist-asked, the banner flag, the SDK's IndexedDB stores)
//   - mail: Scaleway TEM sends sign-in codes; Cloudflare Email Routing receives support@/privacy@
//     and forwards to an inbox at our e-mail provider (provider unnamed: maintainer to confirm)
//
// When the text changes in substance: bump PRIVACY_POLICY_DATE, and move
// CommercePublisherClient.PRIVACY_VERSION and web/apex commerceApi.ts PRIVACY_VERSION with it.
//
// ⚠ Pure: no imports but a type. Plain Node runs it for the support-page build.
